<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-4-defining-class">
  <title>Defining a Class: State and Behavior</title>

  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Now that we have seen the overall structure of a solution with a programmer-defined 
      class, let's take a deeper look at class design and implementation.
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose we are tasked with creating a dashboard to track the status of an electric 
      vehicle (EV). The EV specifications include its model, battery capacity, and its 
      efficiency<mdash/>the distance it can travel per kilowatt-hour. It also has state 
      information: a current charge and an odometer. The EV can be driven, it can be charged, 
      and it can answer questions based on its specifications and current state, such as its 
      model, odometer reading, estimated range, battery percentage, and whether it needs charging.
    </p>

    <p>
      An EV is an excellent candidate for a class. It consists of state (model, battery 
      capacity, efficiency, current charge, current odometer reading), behavior that accesses 
      the state (get model, get odometer, get range, get battery percentage, needs charging), 
      as well as behavior that changes the state (drive, charge).
    </p>
  </introduction>



  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-4-1-class-diagrams">
    <title>Class Diagrams</title>

    <p>
      A class diagram is a quick way to specify and document a class. A class diagram is a 
      structural diagram that is part of the Unified Modeling Language (UML), a general-purpose 
      set of visual notations used to design and document software systems. Here is the class 
      diagram for <c>ElectricVehicle</c>:
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-electric-vehicle-class-diagram">
      <title>Class diagram for <c>ElectricVehicle</c></title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
        <col width="100%"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell halign="center"><strong>ElectricVehicle</strong></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell>
            <p>- model: String</p>
            <p>- batteryCapacityKwh: double</p>
            <p>- milesPerKwh: double</p>
            <p>- currentChargeKwh: double</p>
            <p>- odometerMiles: double</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <p>+ ElectricVehicle(model: String, batteryCapacityKwh: double, milesPerKwh: double)</p>
            <p>+ drive(miles: double): void</p>
            <p>+ charge(kwhAdded: double): void</p>
            <p>+ getEstimatedRange(): double</p>
            <p>+ getBatteryPercentage(): double</p>
            <p>+ needsCharging(): boolean</p>
            <p>+ getModel(): String</p>
            <p>+ getOdometerMiles(): double</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The class diagram is divided into three rows: the name of the class in the first row, 
      the fields in the second row, and the methods in the third row. Other features of the 
      class diagram are:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Visibility:</strong> A dash (<c>-</c>) before a class member (field or method) 
          indicates <c>private</c> visibility. A plus sign (<c>+</c>) indicates <c>public</c> 
          visibility.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Fields and parameters are shown as name, colon, data type (such as <c>model: String</c>).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The return type of a method is shown with a colon and the return type at the end 
          of the method signature.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          You can include an optional fourth row with the object's responsibilities. This is 
          helpful when you are designing a class but have not worked out fields and methods yet. 
          It also provides a summary of the class once the details are worked out.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      We can quickly build the skeleton of our class by following the class diagram. From the 
      first and second rows, we declare our fields:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
public class ElectricVehicle {

    private String model;
    private double batteryCapacityKwh;
    private double milesPerKwh;
    private double currentChargeKwh;
    private double odometerMiles;
    </program>

    <p>
      Note that each field has a dash in front of it in the class diagram, so we specify 
      <c>private</c> visibility in the class definition. We can then fill in the method 
      headers with empty bodies:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    public ElectricVehicle(String model, double batteryCapacityKwh, double milesPerKwh) {
    }

    public void drive(double miles) {
    }

    public void charge(double kwhAdded) {
    }

    public double getEstimatedRange() {
        return 0.0;
    }

    public double getBatteryPercentage() {
        return 0.0;
    }

    public boolean needsCharging() {
        return false;
    }

    public String getModel() {
        return "";
    }

    public double getOdometerMiles() {
        return 0.0;
    } 
}
    </program>

    <p>
      Each of the methods has public visibility as specified with a plus sign in the class 
      diagram, so we put the keyword <c>public</c> as part of each header. Notice that the 
      accessor methods (<c>getEstimatedRange</c>, <c>getBatteryPercentage</c>, <c>needsCharging</c>, 
      <c>getModel</c>, and <c>getOdometerMiles</c>) all have a return type and take no parameters. 
      The mutator methods (<c>drive</c>, <c>charge</c>) are void methods and do take parameters. 
      This is often the pattern for accessors and mutators, but it is not a hard rule.
    </p>

    <p>
      Instance methods operate on the fields of an <c>ElectricVehicle</c> object, so not all 
      data has to come in as parameters or be returned as a result:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          The mutators change the fields of the object<mdash/>the data coming in as parameters 
          is used to change the fields, and there is usually no need to return anything. The 
          object itself has changed after the mutator executes.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The accessors return information about the data stored in the fields, such as whether 
          the vehicle needs charging. We do not need a parameter to come in, because the method 
          is answering a question about the object itself. We do need to return the answer, so 
          the method has a return type.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Sometimes we will write accessors and mutators that do not follow this pattern, but it 
      is an important general difference between writing a class and writing a functionally 
      decomposed program. In a functionally decomposed method, we must pass all the information 
      into the method that it needs to fulfill its responsibilities. When we are writing an 
      instance method, the data is already part of the object that the method was invoked on. 
      In a functionally decomposed method, we might need to return changed values, but in an 
      instance method, the change is made directly in the object's fields.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let's break down the design of <c>ElectricVehicle</c> and write some of the methods.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-4-2-encapsulation-state">
    <title>Encapsulation and Object State</title>

    <p>
      An object is an encapsulation of data and operations on that data. If we write our object 
      properly, the data will always be in an internally consistent, coherent, and meaningful 
      state. What does that mean? If we consider the <c>ElectricVehicle</c> class, the data 
      includes <c>batteryCapacityKwh</c> and <c>currentChargeKwh</c>. An internally consistent 
      and meaningful state requires the following:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>currentChargeKwh</c> must be less than or equal to <c>batteryCapacityKwh</c> 
          (as the charge cannot exceed the battery's physical capacity).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>currentChargeKwh</c> is never less than zero.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      If the <c>currentChargeKwh</c> field were <c>public</c>, an outside entity could simply 
      change that field's value with an assignment statement. It could set the field to a value 
      larger than <c>batteryCapacityKwh</c>, violating the invariant. It could set the field's 
      value to a negative number, violating physical reality. The class would fail in its 
      responsibility to maintain a coherent, meaningful state.
    </p>

    <p>
      These rules are called <em>class invariants</em>. A class invariant is a condition or 
      rule that must always remain true for every valid instance of a class throughout its 
      lifetime<mdash/>from the moment the constructor finishes executing until the object is 
      destroyed.
    </p>

    <p>
      In our design, the <c>currentChargeKwh</c> field is <c>private</c>, and can only be 
      changed through the <c>drive</c> and <c>charge</c> methods. The <c>drive</c> method can 
      validate that the <c>miles</c> parameter passed to <c>drive</c> does not exceed the car's 
      estimated range, ensuring that the battery's current charge is never negative. The 
      <c>charge</c> method can prevent <c>currentChargeKwh</c> from exceeding <c>batteryCapacityKwh</c> 
      by capping off the charge at the maximum value if <c>kwhAdded</c> is more charge than the 
      battery needs (just as in the real world an EV will not absorb more charge than it can 
      handle).
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="listing-ev-drive-charge"/> is an implementation of the <c>drive</c> and 
      <c>charge</c> methods of <c>ElectricVehicle</c>. Pay special attention to the preconditions, 
      postconditions, and assertions:
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-ev-drive-charge">
      <caption>The <c>drive</c> and <c>charge</c> methods of <c>ElectricVehicle</c></caption>
      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    /**
     * Drives the vehicle a specified distance, increasing the odometer and
     * reducing the battery charge accordingly.
     *
     * Precondition: miles > 0.0 &amp;&amp; miles &lt;= getEstimatedRange()
     * Postcondition: odometerMiles increases by miles, and currentChargeKwh
     *                decreases by (miles / milesPerKwh).
     *
     * @param miles the distance to drive in miles
     */
    public void drive(double miles) {
        assert miles > 0.0 : "Distance driven must be positive: " + miles;
        assert miles &lt;= getEstimatedRange() : 
            "Insufficient battery range for trip: " + miles;

        double energyUsed = miles / this.milesPerKwh;
        this.currentChargeKwh -= energyUsed;
        this.odometerMiles += miles;
    }

    /**
     * Adds electrical energy to the battery, capped at the battery's maximum capacity.
     *
     * Precondition: kwhAdded > 0.0
     * Postcondition: currentChargeKwh increases by kwhAdded, up to batteryCapacityKwh.
     *
     * @param kwhAdded the amount of energy added in kilowatt-hours
     */
    public void charge(double kwhAdded) {
        assert kwhAdded > 0.0 : "Charge amount must be positive: " + kwhAdded;
        this.currentChargeKwh = Math.min(this.batteryCapacityKwh,
                                         this.currentChargeKwh + kwhAdded);
    }
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      The <c>drive</c> method takes a <c>miles</c> parameter which is the distance to drive. 
      We have a precondition on that parameter: <c>miles > 0.0 &amp;&amp; miles &lt;= getEstimatedRange()</c>. 
      The <c>getEstimatedRange</c> method returns the distance the vehicle can drive in its current 
      state<mdash/>given the value of <c>currentChargeKwh</c>. Within the method, two <c>assert</c> 
      statements check the precondition and display appropriate error messages. If no assertion 
      throws an error, the postconditions are fulfilled: <c>odometerMiles</c> increases by 
      <c>miles</c>, and <c>currentChargeKwh</c> decreases by <c>miles / milesPerKwh</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Notice that both of these changes are internal to the object<mdash/>they both change object 
      fields. No value is returned, but if the preconditions were met, the battery's current 
      charge is decreased and the odometer value is increased.
    </p>

    <p>
      The <c>charge</c> method takes a <c>kwhAdded</c> parameter representing the amount of energy 
      to add to <c>currentChargeKwh</c>. The method precondition states that this cannot be negative, 
      and an <c>assert</c> throws an error with a meaningful message if the precondition is violated. 
      The method then fulfills the postcondition: it increases the <c>currentChargeKwh</c> field 
      up to but not exceeding the <c>batteryCapacityKwh</c> field. Again, no value is returned: 
      an internal field of the object is modified.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-4-3-fields-constructors">
    <title>Fields and Constructors</title>

    <p>
      An object's fields are the data the object needs to maintain to fulfill its responsibilities. 
      An object can have as many fields as it needs, of any data type, including other objects. 
      The values in the fields comprise the object's state. Another kind of UML diagram is an 
      <em>object diagram</em>, which shows the values in the fields of a specific instance at a 
      specific point during program execution.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="listing-ev-demo"/> is a class with a <c>main</c> method that creates two 
      <c>ElectricVehicle</c> objects. We will examine the state of the <c>commuter</c> and 
      <c>cruiser</c> objects at two points during program execution.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-ev-demo">
      <caption><c>ElectricVehicleDemo</c> creates two objects and invokes methods on them</caption>
      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
public class ElectricVehicleDemo {
    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Declares two electric vehicle variables.
        ElectricVehicle commuter, cruiser;
        
        // Instantiates the ElectricVehicle objects.
        commuter = new ElectricVehicle("Chevy Bolt EV", 65.0, 4.0);
        cruiser = new ElectricVehicle("Hyundai Ioniq 5", 77.4, 3.9);

        // Displays the estimated range for each.
        System.out.printf("%s range: %.1f miles %n", commuter.getModel(), 
                          commuter.getEstimatedRange());
        System.out.printf("%s range: %.1f miles %n", cruiser.getModel(), 
                          cruiser.getEstimatedRange());

        // Drives both vehicles.
        System.out.println("--- Driving ---");
        commuter.drive(100.0);
        cruiser.drive(280.0); 

        // Charges the cruiser and displays its range.
        System.out.printf("%s range: %.1f miles %n", cruiser.getModel(), 
                          cruiser.getEstimatedRange());
        System.out.println("--- Charging ---");
        cruiser.charge(40.0);
        System.out.printf("%s range: %.1f miles %n", cruiser.getModel(), 
                          cruiser.getEstimatedRange());
    }
}
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      In <xref ref="listing-ev-demo"/>, the <c>commuter</c> and <c>cruiser</c> objects are 
      created on lines 9 and 10. On these lines, space is allocated for the objects on the heap 
      and their constructors are invoked. Note again that a constructor does not have a return 
      type, and it has the exact same name as the class. When we create an object, we write:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
variable = new Class(arguments);
    </program>

    <p>
      This syntax feels natural, as if we are telling Java we want to make a new <c>Class</c> 
      (whatever that class is): a new <c>Point</c>, a new <c>ElectricVehicle</c>, a new <c>File</c>, 
      or a new <c>Scanner</c>. Now you know that this line is invoking a constructor<mdash/>a 
      method with the name of the class that gives the object its initial values.
    </p>

    <p>
      The purpose of a constructor is to put an object into a stable and meaningful initial state. 
      Once the constructor has finished executing, it should be possible to invoke any method on 
      the object, either transforming it to a different (still consistent and meaningful) state, 
      or returning information about the object's current state.
    </p>

    <p>
      After lines 9 and 10 have executed, the object diagrams for <c>commuter</c> and <c>cruiser</c> 
      show their initial state:
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="auto">
      <tabular halign="left" top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
        <col width="100%"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell halign="center"><strong><u>commuter:ElectricVehicle</u></strong></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <p>model = "Chevy Bolt EV"</p>
            <p>batteryCapacityKwh = 65.0</p>
            <p>milesPerKwh = 4.0</p>
            <p>currentChargeKwh = 65.0</p>
            <p>odometerMiles = 0.0</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <tabular halign="left" top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
        <col width="100%"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell halign="center"><strong><u>cruiser:ElectricVehicle</u></strong></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <p>model = "Hyundai Ioniq 5"</p>
            <p>batteryCapacityKwh = 77.4</p>
            <p>milesPerKwh = 3.9</p>
            <p>currentChargeKwh = 77.4</p>
            <p>odometerMiles = 0.0</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      After the constructor has executed, every field of the <c>ElectricVehicle</c> object has 
      a meaningful value, and all values are internally consistent (the class invariants hold). 
      Some of the fields are initialized from values passed into the constructor: <c>model</c>, 
      <c>batteryCapacityKwh</c>, and <c>milesPerKwh</c>. The <c>odometerMiles</c> begins with 
      a value of <c>0.0</c>, and <c>currentChargeKwh</c> is initialized to fully charged, which 
      is the same value as <c>batteryCapacityKwh</c>. Any method can now be invoked on the 
      object and it will be able to respond in a meaningful way based on its state.
    </p>

    <p>
      On line 20, the <c>drive</c> method is invoked on the <c>commuter</c> vehicle with an 
      argument of 100.0, and on line 21, the <c>drive</c> method is invoked on the <c>cruiser</c> 
      object with an argument of 280.0. After these lines of code execute, the objects' states 
      have changed, as shown in these updated object diagrams:
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="auto">
      <tabular halign="left" top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
        <col width="100%"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell halign="center"><strong><u>commuter:ElectricVehicle</u></strong></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <p>model = "Chevy Bolt EV"</p>
            <p>batteryCapacityKwh = 65.0</p>
            <p>milesPerKwh = 4.0</p>
            <p>currentChargeKwh = 40.0</p>
            <p>odometerMiles = 100.0</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <tabular halign="left" top="medium" bottom="medium" left="medium" right="medium">
        <col width="100%"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell halign="center"><strong><u>cruiser:ElectricVehicle</u></strong></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <p>model = "Hyundai Ioniq 5"</p>
            <p>batteryCapacityKwh = 77.4</p>
            <p>milesPerKwh = 3.9</p>
            <p>currentChargeKwh = 5.605</p>
            <p>odometerMiles = 280.0</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      The <c>drive</c> method changes internal state by modifying both the <c>odometerMiles</c> 
      and <c>currentChargeKwh</c> fields. As a program executes and mutator messages are sent to 
      objects, their internal state updates dynamically. Their actions in response to method 
      invocations will be meaningful relative to their current state. For example, before line 21 
      executes, the <c>cruiser</c> object has a <c>currentChargeKwh</c> of 77.4. If we invoked 
      <c>needsCharging</c> on it, it would return <c>false</c>. After line 21, the <c>currentChargeKwh</c> 
      value drops to 5.605, and <c>needsCharging</c> would return <c>true</c> if invoked.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can also observe the internal state of your objects as a program is running by placing 
      a breakpoint in the code and running it in your IDE debugger. <xref ref="fig-ev-variables-debugger"/> 
      is a snapshot of the <c>commuter</c> and <c>cruiser</c> objects in the Variables pane in 
      Eclipse after line 21 has executed. The values are identical to those shown in the object diagrams.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ev-variables-debugger">
      <caption><c>commuter</c> and <c>cruiser</c> after line 21 has executed.</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_3.png" width="90%">
        <description suppress="yes"/>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </subsection>
</section>